WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:01.970
- Thank you Irene.

00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:03.468
Dr. David Poulin is a physicist

00:00:03.468 --> 00:00:07.850
whose research focuses
primarily on finding efficient,

00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:11.670
effective methods to
protect quantum information

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:13.510
and this is of course an anticipation

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:17.190
of the quantum computing revolution

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:19.040
that's already begun

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:22.070
where quantum computing
devices will change

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:25.010
the face of computation as we know it.

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:27.320
Protecting information in that area

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:30.930
is not surprisingly gonna
be as much of a challenge

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:32.390
as it is today.

00:00:32.390 --> 00:00:35.110
And the fact that CIFAR
provides opportunities

00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:36.670
for young researchers to mix

00:00:36.670 --> 00:00:40.230
with experienced members in their field,

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:43.120
led Dr. Poulin to a situation a while ago.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:45.360
Rarely afforded young researchers

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:49.720
at a CIFAR meeting he said
some provocative things

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:52.810
about quantum mechanics
which prompted a challenge

00:00:52.810 --> 00:00:54.123
from a Nobel Laureate.

00:00:55.130 --> 00:00:56.373
I wasn't there.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:00.830
Dr. Poulin apparently responded directly

00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:05.450
and survived and he's now co-director

00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:08.440
of the Quantum Information
Science program.

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:10.983
Please welcome Dr. David Poulin.

00:01:10.983 --> 00:01:13.733
(crowd clapping)

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:21.160
- Thank you Jay for the introduction.

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:23.120
So, I'm gonna describe to you

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:25.423
what my day in the office looks like.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:28.512
There you go.

00:01:28.512 --> 00:01:31.560
So, when I come in my office

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:34.370
and let's say I don't have
any pressing obligations,

00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:36.210
I like to just stand at the board

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:37.750
and scribble some equations

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:42.540
and these help me come up
with new quantum software.

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:46.570
So, this is what my board
looked like earlier this week.

00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:47.490
In this example,

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:50.270
I was considering a very common task

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:52.850
in machine learning called
stochastic gradient descent.

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:54.370
We're using a lot of computer power

00:01:54.370 --> 00:01:56.740
to solve that particular problem currently

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:58.190
and I was trying to figure out

00:01:58.190 --> 00:02:01.630
if this problem could benefit
from a quantum computer

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:04.540
if we could accelerate this and I failed,

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:05.650
I haven't succeeded,

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:08.820
but I have a couple of
other tricks down my sleeve

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:11.140
and this is just one of many problems

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:12.453
that keep me busy.

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:15.010
But over the years

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:18.480
I have designed a few quantum algorithms,

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:20.340
a few piece of quantum software

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:23.140
that should enable a
future quantum computer

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:25.940
to solve certain computational problems

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:29.340
in a time that is very reasonable,

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:31.500
but that currently cannot be solved

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:33.800
even with the world's
largest supercomputer.

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:37.210
So, if we succeed one day in
building a quantum computer,

00:02:37.210 --> 00:02:39.680
some of the software I came up with

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:43.780
could be used to understand
some complex molecular reaction

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:47.560
or maybe some piece of quantum material

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:48.940
or advanced material

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:52.240
and this in turn could
turn into new drug design

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:53.950
or new energy transport.

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:54.783
I don't know.

00:02:54.783 --> 00:02:55.616
And honestly,

00:02:55.616 --> 00:02:56.449
I don't really care.

00:02:56.449 --> 00:02:58.290
You see, when I do research,

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:01.990
I'm not by these potential applications.

00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:05.070
I just wanna understand
the fundamental power

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:07.853
that quantum mechanics
offers in terms of computing.

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:11.670
And sometimes I work on a problem

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:15.750
and I realize that this
problem cannot possibly benefit

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:17.750
from a quantum computational speed up.

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:19.970
And this might be surprising to you,

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:22.780
but in terms of personal fulfillment,

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:25.210
this is equally rewarding

00:03:25.210 --> 00:03:27.090
as finding a new application

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:28.460
because in both case,

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:30.995
I learned something new
about quantum mechanics.

00:03:30.995 --> 00:03:34.450
So, I think this is possibly the hallmark

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:36.340
of intellectual freedom.

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:40.240
It's having the luxury or the freedom

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:43.070
or the privilege of asking a question

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:44.980
without a priori,

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:45.900
any preference

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:47.230
or what the answer should be.

00:03:47.230 --> 00:03:49.346
We just wanna know the answer.

00:03:49.346 --> 00:03:52.760
So, of course coming up
with new quantum software

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:54.420
and new quantum algorithms

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:56.590
requires some technical
skills and knowledge.

00:03:56.590 --> 00:03:59.597
You need to understand how
a quantum computer operates

00:03:59.597 --> 00:04:04.597
and what are the underlying
logic quantum information.

00:04:06.015 --> 00:04:07.290
But I think that

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:11.630
by far the most important
ingredient is creative thinking.

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:15.290
So, you have to take a problem

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:17.630
that we use computers to solve

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:19.810
and understand the
mathematical structure of it,

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:23.160
and try to understand how
you can grab this problem

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:26.096
and make it amenable
to a quantum computer.

00:04:26.096 --> 00:04:28.800
(clears throat)

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:31.443
So, in fact,

00:04:34.579 --> 00:04:38.523
the creative thinking is
not only important today,

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:44.280
the very idea of building
a quantum computer began

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:47.130
when a few very marginal researchers

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:50.530
had the courage of asking
a very bold question.

00:04:50.530 --> 00:04:52.683
The the question they
ask is the following,

00:04:55.901 --> 00:04:59.640
quantum mechanics is this
very counterintuitive theory

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:01.927
that explains the atomic
and subatomic world.

00:05:01.927 --> 00:05:04.097
"So, how can quantum mechanics change

00:05:04.097 --> 00:05:05.727
"the way in which we exchange

00:05:05.727 --> 00:05:07.940
"and process information?"

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:11.460
So, to understand just how
crazy this question is,

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:14.170
you need to know that
quantum mechanics at always

00:05:14.170 --> 00:05:16.871
been restricted to the atomic scale, okay.

00:05:16.871 --> 00:05:18.760
You don't need quantum mechanics

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:22.340
to explain how a tennis
ball will bounce around

00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:24.100
or how airplanes fly.

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:27.660
These are explained by
Newtonian classical mechanics.

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:30.890
So, suggesting that a human scale system

00:05:30.890 --> 00:05:35.890
like a computer could
utilize the strange features

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:38.960
of the quantum universe was delusional

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:42.470
when it was first suggested in the 1980s.

00:05:42.470 --> 00:05:46.000
Yet today we have major
corporations, government

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:49.040
and venture capitalists
investing billions of dollars

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:50.960
to build a quantum computer.

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:54.100
This picture shows IBM's latest attempt.

00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:55.160
You can even play with it.

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:57.510
You could access it on
the cloud if you'd like.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:02.200
So, of course these industrial players

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:06.060
are primarily interested in
harnessing... in harvesting,

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:06.893
sorry,

00:06:06.893 --> 00:06:08.150
the tremendous computational power

00:06:08.150 --> 00:06:10.010
of quantum information,

00:06:10.010 --> 00:06:13.890
but they also recognize that
without intellectual freedom,

00:06:13.890 --> 00:06:15.940
this power would never
have been unleashed.

00:06:15.940 --> 00:06:16.773
Okay.

00:06:16.773 --> 00:06:18.610
So, organizations like CIFAR

00:06:18.610 --> 00:06:22.170
help us stay vigilant
at protecting our right

00:06:22.170 --> 00:06:25.510
to ask very tricky and risky questions.

00:06:25.510 --> 00:06:27.570
And our CIFAR program,

00:06:27.570 --> 00:06:29.790
the Quantum Information Science program

00:06:29.790 --> 00:06:33.703
has been largely shaped by
another very risky question.

00:06:35.450 --> 00:06:36.283
The question is,

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:39.970
what do we learn about fundamental physics

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:43.050
by thinking about physical systems,

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:45.020
whether it's an airplane,

00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:46.980
this remote or a black hole

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:51.002
or whatnot as an
information processing unit?

00:06:51.002 --> 00:06:53.830
So, this may sound like a strange question

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:55.423
and it deserves explanation.

00:06:56.270 --> 00:06:59.400
So, of course we're used
to think of information

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:01.030
as something immaterial.

00:07:01.030 --> 00:07:03.900
Think about the password to my smartphone.

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:06.200
This has nothing to do
with physics, does it?

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:08.320
But the reality is that

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:11.250
any information requires
a physical system,

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:13.152
whether it's my brain,

00:07:13.152 --> 00:07:17.380
a hard drive in my phone
or ink on a piece of paper,

00:07:17.380 --> 00:07:20.130
there can be no information
without physical support.

00:07:20.130 --> 00:07:21.793
And this has consequences.

00:07:23.350 --> 00:07:27.520
So, to give you a simple example,

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:30.080
you know that no physical
body can travel faster

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:31.530
than the speed of light.

00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:33.930
Therefore, a consequence is that

00:07:33.930 --> 00:07:35.650
you cannot send information,

00:07:35.650 --> 00:07:37.660
information cannot be transmitted faster

00:07:37.660 --> 00:07:38.780
than the speed of light

00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:41.420
because information needs to be supported

00:07:41.420 --> 00:07:43.340
by physical system.

00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:45.380
So, this is an example of how

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:48.390
the laws of physics impose
constraints on the laws

00:07:48.390 --> 00:07:50.720
and the rules of information.

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:52.280
But what we like to do now is

00:07:52.280 --> 00:07:54.247
turn this picture around and say,

00:07:54.247 --> 00:07:57.727
"How do the rules of
information processing affect

00:07:57.727 --> 00:07:59.447
"the universe in which we live?"

00:08:00.570 --> 00:08:04.210
The upshot of this is that
information is universal.

00:08:04.210 --> 00:08:07.790
So, if you discover that
there's a fundamental limit

00:08:07.790 --> 00:08:10.580
on some information processing task,

00:08:10.580 --> 00:08:12.390
this puts a limit of physical

00:08:12.390 --> 00:08:15.090
and constraint on every
possible embodiment

00:08:15.090 --> 00:08:16.660
of that information.

00:08:16.660 --> 00:08:18.950
So, information theory is really universal

00:08:18.950 --> 00:08:20.890
and it cuts across physics.

00:08:20.890 --> 00:08:22.520
So, you could be studying,

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:25.900
say electronic correlation
in a piece of superconductor,

00:08:25.900 --> 00:08:28.820
and you discover a new
information pattern there,

00:08:28.820 --> 00:08:30.900
and you can transpose
this information pattern

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:32.410
to something like a black hole

00:08:32.410 --> 00:08:35.393
and learn entire new physics there.

00:08:36.500 --> 00:08:37.810
And I'm not just saying that

00:08:37.810 --> 00:08:40.140
because that example I just gave

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:42.050
was one of the crown achievement

00:08:42.050 --> 00:08:44.710
of our CIFAR program
in the past five year.

00:08:44.710 --> 00:08:48.230
We took the knowledge we
have of quantum information

00:08:49.250 --> 00:08:52.120
to shed new light on some
of the deepest questions

00:08:52.120 --> 00:08:55.660
in physics including the black
hole evaporation paradox.

00:08:55.660 --> 00:08:57.450
This is where the two pillars

00:08:57.450 --> 00:08:59.370
of modern physics quantum mechanics

00:08:59.370 --> 00:09:01.383
and general relativity collide.

00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:05.763
So, enough may be about
these achievements.

00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:10.020
So, you all know that
information technologies

00:09:10.020 --> 00:09:13.090
have transformed our
society in very deep way.

00:09:13.090 --> 00:09:16.390
It's also transformed the
way we pursue knowledge.

00:09:16.390 --> 00:09:19.370
And I believe that this
trend will continue.

00:09:19.370 --> 00:09:22.857
I wouldn't be surprised that soon

00:09:22.857 --> 00:09:27.430
the information theoretic
perspective will affect many

00:09:27.430 --> 00:09:29.840
if not all areas of science,

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:32.920
and maybe it will even blur

00:09:32.920 --> 00:09:37.640
the boundary between
scientific disciplines.

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:39.530
The way we currently organize the research

00:09:39.530 --> 00:09:40.667
is by saying,

00:09:40.667 --> 00:09:43.557
"Oh, this is humanity,

00:09:43.557 --> 00:09:44.767
"this is life science,

00:09:44.767 --> 00:09:46.830
"this is physical science,

00:09:46.830 --> 00:09:48.267
but maybe in the future,

00:09:48.267 --> 00:09:50.277
"we will organize research in terms of

00:09:50.277 --> 00:09:52.077
"what information processing

00:09:52.077 --> 00:09:54.150
"is this research concerned with."

00:09:54.150 --> 00:09:56.940
So, here's maybe some information,

00:09:56.940 --> 00:09:58.390
some correlation structure

00:09:58.390 --> 00:10:00.450
that shows up in the human genome

00:10:00.450 --> 00:10:03.080
and the same pattern
shows up in economics.

00:10:03.080 --> 00:10:04.850
So, these people should
be working together

00:10:04.850 --> 00:10:07.600
because in terms of
information perspective,

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:10.010
they're working on the same problem.

00:10:10.010 --> 00:10:10.860
So, you might think that

00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:12.840
this kind of idea sounds completely crazy

00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:14.280
but that's precisely why I'm here.

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:15.250
So, thank you,
(crowd laughs)

00:10:15.250 --> 00:10:16.450
and have a good evening.

